// ─── DSA Interview Questions (200) ──────────────────────────────────────
export const DSA_QUESTIONS = [
  // ── Easy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is a data structure and why is it important?", "easy", "A data structure is a way of organizing and storing data to enable efficient access and modification. Examples: arrays (contiguous memory), linked lists (non-contiguous nodes), trees (hierarchical), hash tables (key-value). Choosing the right data structure directly impacts algorithm performance — O(1) hash map lookup vs O(n) array search. Without proper data structures, even simple operations become inefficient at scale."],
  ["What is an algorithm?", "easy", "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure for solving a problem or completing a task. Has: input, output, definiteness (clear steps), finiteness (terminates), effectiveness (each step doable). Examples: sorting (merge sort, quicksort), searching (binary search), graph traversal (BFS, DFS). Algorithm analysis uses time/space complexity (Big O)."],
  ["What is Big O notation?", "easy", "Big O describes the upper bound of algorithm runtime or memory usage as input size grows. Expresses WORST-CASE complexity. Common classes: O(1) (constant — hash lookup), O(log n) (logarithmic — binary search), O(n) (linear — array traversal), O(n log n) (linearithmic — merge sort), O(n²) (quadratic — bubble sort), O(2^n) (exponential — naive Fibonacci). Used to compare algorithm efficiency independent of hardware."],
  ["What is O(1), O(n), O(n²) time complexity?", "easy", "O(1): constant time — operation takes the same time regardless of input size (array access by index, hash map lookup). O(n): linear time — time grows proportionally to input size (finding max in array, linear search). O(n²): quadratic time — time grows with square of input (nested loops, bubble sort). O(n²) becomes impractical for large n (10K items → 100M operations)."],
  ["What is space complexity?", "easy", "Space complexity measures the total memory an algorithm uses relative to input size. Includes: input storage, auxiliary space (temporary variables, recursion stack), output storage. Example: merge sort uses O(n) auxiliary space (temporary arrays); in-place quicksort uses O(log n) (recursion stack). Trade-off: sometimes using more space improves time (memoization reduces exponential to polynomial)."],
  ["What is an array? What is its time complexity for access, insert, delete?", "easy", "Array: contiguous memory block storing elements of the same type. Access: O(1) (index-based, direct memory address). Insert at end (amortized): O(1). Insert at beginning/middle: O(n) (shift elements). Delete from end: O(1). Delete from beginning/middle: O(n) (shift elements). Arrays have fixed size (static) or dynamic (resize when full, amortized O(1) append). Cache-friendly due to locality of reference."],
  ["What is the difference between an array and a linked list?", "easy", "Array: contiguous memory, O(1) index access, O(n) insert/delete (except at end). Cache-friendly. Fixed size (or dynamic with resizing). Linked List: non-contiguous nodes with pointers, O(n) access (must traverse), O(1) insert/delete if you have the node reference. No cache locality. Dynamic size. Choose array for: frequent random access, small fixed-size data. Choose linked list for: frequent insert/delete, unknown size."],
  ["What is a singly linked list?", "easy", "Singly linked list: each node has data + pointer to NEXT node. Last node points to null. Head points to first node. Operations: search O(n), insert at head O(1), insert at tail O(n) (unless tail pointer), delete O(n). Can only traverse forward (no prev pointer). Used for: stacks, adjacency lists, hash map collision chains."],
  ["What is a doubly linked list?", "easy", "Doubly linked list: each node has data + prev pointer + next pointer. Extra memory per node (pointer overhead) vs singly linked. Advantages: O(1) delete given a node (don't need previous), bidirectional traversal, O(1) insert at both ends. Used in: LRU cache (O(1) move-to-front), browser history (back/forward), deque implementation."],
  ["What is a stack? What are its operations?", "easy", "Stack: LIFO (Last In, First Out) data structure. Operations: push (add to top) O(1), pop (remove from top) O(1), peek (view top without removing) O(1), isEmpty O(1). Implemented with array or linked list. Uses: function call stack (recursion), undo/redo, expression evaluation (postfix), DFS traversal, bracket matching."],
  ["What is a queue? What are its operations?", "easy", "Queue: FIFO (First In, First Out) data structure. Operations: enqueue (add to back) O(1), dequeue (remove from front) O(1), peek (view front) O(1), isEmpty O(1). Implement with: array (circular buffer), linked list, or two stacks. Variants: priority queue (heap-based), deque (double-ended), circular queue. Uses: BFS, printer spooling, task scheduling, message queuing."],
  ["What is the difference between a stack and a queue?", "easy", "Stack: LIFO — last element added is first removed. Queue: FIFO — first element added is first removed. Stack push/pop at same end (top). Queue enqueue at back, dequeue at front. Stack is vertical (like a stack of plates); queue is horizontal (like a line of people). Stack uses: DFS, undo. Queue uses: BFS, scheduling."],
  ["What is LIFO and FIFO?", "easy", "LIFO (Last In, First Out): the most recently added item is removed first. Analogy: stack of plates. FIFO (First In, First Out): the earliest added item is removed first. Analogy: queue at a ticket counter. LIFO → stack data structure. FIFO → queue data structure."],
  ["What is a hash table / hash map?", "easy", "Hash table: key-value data structure using a hash function to compute an index into an array of buckets. Average: O(1) insert, O(1) lookup, O(1) delete. Worst-case: O(n) (many collisions). Collision resolution: chaining (linked list per bucket) or open addressing (linear probing, quadratic probing, double hashing). Load factor = items / buckets — resize when exceeds threshold (e.g., 0.75). Used for: caches, dictionaries, database indexing, symbol tables."],
  ["What is a hash function?", "easy", "Hash function maps a key (any data) to an integer index within the hash table's range. Good hash functions: deterministic (same key → same hash), uniform distribution (avoids clustering), fast to compute. Examples: modulo for integers, polynomial for strings, cryptographic (SHA-256 for security). Poor hash functions cause collisions (different keys mapping to same index). Java: hashCode(), Python: hash()."],
  ["What is a collision in a hash table?", "easy", "Collision: two different keys hash to the same bucket index. Resolved via: 1) Chaining: each bucket holds a linked list of entries; collisions → add to list. 2) Open addressing: find next empty slot (linear probing +1, quadratic probing +i², double hashing). Chaining is simpler; open addressing is more cache-friendly. Load factor affects collision probability — lower load = fewer collisions."],
  ["What is linear search and its time complexity?", "easy", "Linear search: iterate through array sequentially until target found. Time: O(n) average/worst (check n elements). O(1) best (first element). Space: O(1). Works on unsorted data. Simple but inefficient for large datasets. When data is unsorted, linear search is the only option (binary search requires sorted data)."],
  ["What is binary search and its time complexity?", "easy", "Binary search: repeatedly divides sorted array in half, comparing middle element to target. Time: O(log n) — logarithmic. Space: O(1) iterative, O(log n) recursive (stack). Requires SORTED array. Fast even for large n (log₂ 1M ≈ 20 comparisons). Variants: find first/last occurrence, find insertion point. Implementation: two-pointer iterative (recommended) or recursive."],
  ["What is a prerequisite for binary search?", "easy", "The array must be SORTED. Binary search relies on the sorted property to eliminate half the search space each iteration. If data is unsorted: sort first (O(n log n)), or use linear search. For dynamic data (frequent insertions): use BST (O(log n) search without sorting)."],
  ["What is a tree data structure?", "easy", "Tree: hierarchical data structure with nodes connected by edges. Root (top), parent, child, leaf (no children), subtree. Binary tree: each node has ≤2 children. Binary search tree: left < parent < right. Tree height: number of edges from root to deepest leaf. Balanced tree: height O(log n). Uses: file systems, DOM, routing algorithms, decision trees, databases (B-tree, B+ tree)."],
  ["What is a binary tree?", "easy", "Binary tree: each node has at most 2 children (left, right). Types: full (0 or 2 children), complete (all levels filled except possibly last), perfect (all internal nodes have 2 children, all leaves same level), balanced (height O(log n)), skewed (degenerate, like linked list). Traversals: preorder (root, left, right), inorder (left, root, right — gives sorted order for BST), postorder (left, right, root), level order (BFS)."],
  ["What is a binary search tree (BST)?", "easy", "BST: binary tree where for each node: left subtree values < node value < right subtree values. Operations: search O(h), insert O(h), delete O(h), where h = tree height. Balanced BST: h = O(log n), operations O(log n). Skewed BST: h = O(n), operations O(n). Inorder traversal gives sorted order. Used for: sorted data maintenance, range queries, database indexing."],
  ["What is the difference between a tree and a graph?", "easy", "Tree: acyclic (no cycles), connected, exactly one path between any two nodes, one root, hierarchical. Graph: may have cycles, may be disconnected, multiple paths possible, any node can be start, no hierarchy. Trees are a subset of graphs (connected acyclic graphs). Tree: parent-child relationships. Graph: arbitrary connections (edges)."],
  ["What is recursion?", "easy", "Recursion: function calls itself to solve smaller instances of the same problem. Components: base case (terminating condition, prevents infinite recursion) and recursive case (calls itself on smaller input). Examples: factorial, Fibonacci, tree traversal, merge sort. Stack depth limited — excessive recursion causes stack overflow. Iterative solutions avoid stack overflow but may be less intuitive."],
  ["What is a base case in recursion?", "easy", "Base case: the simplest instance of the problem that can be solved directly without recursion. It stops the recursion. Without a base case, the function calls itself infinitely (stack overflow). Example (factorial): if n ≤ 1 return 1 (base case), else return n * factorial(n-1) (recursive case). Each recursive call should move toward the base case."],
  ["What is a palindrome? How do you check if a string is one?", "easy", "Palindrome: string that reads same forward and backward ('racecar', 'madam', 'A man a plan a canal Panama' ignoring case/spaces). Check: two-pointer (i=0, j=n-1, compare while i<j), or reverse string and compare. Time: O(n), Space: O(1) (two-pointer) or O(n) (reverse)."],
  ["How do you reverse a string?", "easy", "Two-pointer: convert to array, swap i with n-1-i, increment i, decrement j. Time O(n), Space O(n) (array) or O(1) (in-place if mutable). Built-in: str.split('').reverse().join('') (JS). String immutability: most languages require converting to array first."],
  ["How do you find the maximum element in an array?", "easy", "Linear scan: iterate once, track max. Time O(n), Space O(1). Initialize max = arr[0], for each element: if arr[i] > max, update max. Works for unsorted arrays. Edge case: empty array (return undefined or -Infinity)."],
  ["How do you check if two strings are anagrams?", "easy", "Anagram: same characters, different order ('listen'/'silent'). Methods: 1) Sort both strings, compare (O(n log n)), 2) Character frequency count with hash map or array of 26 (O(n)) — increment for s1, decrement for s2, check all zero. Method 2 is optimal O(n)."],
  ["What is the two-pointer technique?", "easy", "Two pointers: use two indices to traverse an array from different directions/speeds. Common patterns: opposite ends (pair sum in sorted array, palindrome check), fast/slow (detect cycle in linked list, find middle), sliding window (fixed/expanding window). Reduces time complexity from O(n²) to O(n) for many problems. Must understand pointer movement conditions."],

  // ── Medium ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is bubble sort and its time complexity?", "med", "Bubble sort: repeatedly swaps adjacent elements if in wrong order. Each 'pass' places largest element at end. Time: O(n²) average/worst, O(n) best (already sorted with optimization — no swaps → stop). Space: O(1) in-place. Simple but inefficient. Rarely used in practice (except educational). Optimized: track if any swaps made in inner loop — exit early if none."],
  ["What is selection sort and its time complexity?", "med", "Selection sort: find minimum element, swap with first position. Find next minimum from remaining, swap with second position. Repeat. Time: O(n²) always (best/worst/average — no early exit). Space: O(1) in-place. Unstable sort (may change relative order of equal elements). Simple but always O(n²) — worse than insertion sort (which has O(n) best case)."],
  ["What is insertion sort and when is it efficient?", "med", "Insertion sort: builds sorted array one element at a time by inserting each element into its correct position among previously sorted elements. Time: O(n²) average/worst, O(n) best (already sorted). Space: O(1) in-place. Efficient for: small datasets (n < 50), nearly sorted data, online algorithms (data arrives incrementally). Used as base case in Timsort (Python/Java sort). Stable sort."],
  ["What is merge sort and its time complexity?", "med", "Merge sort: divide array in half, recursively sort each half, merge sorted halves. Time: O(n log n) for all cases (best/worst/average). Space: O(n) auxiliary (temporary arrays for merge). Stable sort. Advantages: guaranteed O(n log n), works well on linked lists (O(1) space), parallelizable. Disadvantage: O(n) extra space. Divide and conquer algorithm."],
  ["What is quicksort and its average and worst case?", "med", "Quicksort: pick pivot, partition array (elements < pivot left, > pivot right), recursively sort partitions. Average: O(n log n). Worst: O(n²) (already sorted with bad pivot selection, e.g., first/last element). Avoid worst case: random pivot, median-of-three. Space: O(log n) (recursion stack). In-place (most implementations). Unstable. Fastest in practice for arrays (good cache performance). Used in C++, Java (Dual-Pivot)."],
  ["What is heap sort?", "med", "Heap sort: build max-heap, repeatedly extract root (max), swap with last element, heapify down. Time: O(n log n) — guaranteed (no worst-case). O(n) to build heap, O(n log n) for extraction. Space: O(1) in-place. Unstable. Slower than quicksort in practice (poor cache locality — jumps around memory). Advantages: no worst case, in-place."],
  ["What is a stable vs unstable sorting algorithm?", "med", "Stable sort: maintains relative order of equal elements. If two items have same key, their original order is preserved. Unstable: equal elements may be reordered. Stable: merge sort, insertion sort, bubble sort, Timsort. Unstable: quicksort, heap sort, selection sort. Stability matters when: sorting by multiple criteria (sort by date, then by name), or maintaining row associations."],
  ["What is in-place sorting?", "med", "In-place: sorts using O(1) or O(log n) extra space (constant or logarithmic additional memory, not counting input). Examples: quicksort (O(log n) recursion stack is considered in-place), heap sort (O(1)), insertion sort (O(1)), bubble sort (O(1)). Not in-place: merge sort (O(n) auxiliary), counting sort (O(k) auxiliary for k range). In-place is memory-efficient for large datasets."],
  ["What is a min-heap and max-heap?", "med", "Heap: complete binary tree where parent dominates children. Min-heap: parent ≤ children (root = minimum). Max-heap: parent ≥ children (root = maximum). Operations: insert O(log n), extract min/max O(log n), peek O(1), heapify O(n). Array representation: parent at i, children at 2i+1 and 2i+2. Used for: priority queues, heap sort, Dijkstra's algorithm, Top-K problems."],
  ["What is a priority queue?", "med", "Priority queue: abstract data type where elements have priorities — highest priority removed first. Operations: insert (priority, element), extractMax (remove highest priority), peekMax (view without removing). Implemented with: binary heap (most common O(log n) insert/extract), Fibonacci heap (O(1) insert), sorted array (O(1) extract, O(n) insert). Uses: Dijkstra's, A* search, Huffman coding, task scheduling."],
  ["What is BFS (Breadth-First Search)?", "med", "BFS: graph traversal that explores neighbors level by level. Uses queue. O(V + E) time, O(V) space (queue + visited set). Pseudocode: queue = [start], visited = {start}, while queue: node = queue.dequeue(), process, enqueue unvisited neighbors. Finds SHORTEST PATH in unweighted graphs. Applications: shortest path, web crawling, social network friend suggestions, garbage collection (Cheney's algorithm)."],
  ["What is DFS (Depth-First Search)?", "med", "DFS: graph traversal that explores as far as possible before backtracking. Uses stack (recursion or explicit). O(V + E) time, O(V) space (recursion stack or explicit stack). Recursive: visit(node), mark visited, for each neighbor if not visited: DFS(neighbor). Applications: topological sort, cycle detection, connected components, maze solving, puzzle solving (maze, sudoku)."],
  ["What is the difference between BFS and DFS?", "med", "BFS: uses queue, explores level-by-level, shortest path in unweighted graphs, O(V+E) time, O(V) space (queue). DFS: uses stack/recursion, explores depth-first, not guaranteed shortest path, O(V+E) time, O(V) space (stack). BFS better for: shortest path, closer neighbors. DFS better for: tree traversals (pre/in/post order), topological sort, detecting cycles, path existence."],
  ["What are the three types of DFS tree traversal? (pre, in, post order)", "med", "Preorder (root → left → right): process root, then left subtree, then right. Used for: copying tree, prefix notation, serialization. Inorder (left → root → right): process left subtree, root, right subtree. For BST: gives sorted order. Postorder (left → right → root): process left, right, then root. Used for: tree deletion, postfix notation, computing subtree sizes."],
  ["What is level-order traversal of a binary tree?", "med", "Level-order (BFS): visit nodes level by level, left to right. Uses queue: enqueue root, while queue not empty: dequeue, process, enqueue left (if exists), enqueue right (if exists). Time O(n), Space O(n) (max queue size = max width at any level). Applications: breadth-first search in trees, printing tree by levels, finding minimum depth."],
  ["What is a balanced binary tree?", "med", "Balanced tree: height is O(log n) relative to number of nodes. Ensures operations (search, insert, delete) remain O(log n) worst-case. Balancing prevents degenerate case (skewed tree = O(n)). Self-balancing trees: AVL (strict balance, height diff ≤ 1), Red-Black (weaker balance, faster inserts/deletes), B-tree (multi-way). Balance factor = height(left) - height(right)."],
  ["What is an AVL tree?", "med", "AVL tree: self-balancing BST where height difference between left and right subtrees ≤ 1 (balance factor: -1, 0, 1). After insert/delete: check balance factors, perform rotations (single left, single right, double left-right, double right-left) to restore balance. Height: ≈ 1.44 log₂(n). Search: O(log n) guaranteed. Insert/Delete: O(log n). More strictly balanced than Red-Black (slower inserts/deletes, faster lookups)."],
  ["What is a Red-Black tree?", "med", "Red-Black tree: self-balancing BST with color property (red or black) per node. Rules: root black, no adjacent reds, all root-to-leaf paths have same number of black nodes. Height: ≤ 2 log₂(n+1). Operations: O(log n). Used in: Java TreeMap, Linux kernel scheduler, C++ std::map/std::set. Less strict than AVL (faster inserts/deletes, slightly slower lookups)."],
  ["What is a trie (prefix tree)?", "med", "Trie: tree structure for storing strings where each node represents a character. Root is empty. Shared prefixes = common ancestors. Space: O(total characters stored) — can be large (each node has 26+ children pointers). Search: O(m) where m = key length (independent of total entries). Used for: autocomplete, spell checking, IP routing (longest prefix match), dictionary word lookup. Compressed trie (radix tree) reduces memory."],
  ["What is a graph? Name the types.", "med", "Graph: G(V, E) — set of vertices (V) and edges (E) connecting them. Types: directed (edges have direction, u→v), undirected (bidirectional), weighted (edges have weights), unweighted, cyclic/acyclic, connected/disconnected, sparse (few edges, E ≈ V), dense (many edges, E ≈ V²). Special: tree (connected acyclic undirected graph), DAG (directed acyclic graph, for topological sort)."],
  ["What is an adjacency matrix vs adjacency list?", "med", "Adjacency matrix: V×V boolean/weight matrix. adj[i][j] = 1 if edge i→j. O(V²) space. O(1) edge lookup. Good for dense graphs. Simple but memory-heavy. Adjacency list: array of lists — each vertex has list of neighbors. O(V+E) space. O(V) worst-case edge lookup (scan list) — use Set for O(1). Good for sparse graphs (most real-world graphs). Adjacency list is the more common representation."],
  ["What is a directed vs undirected graph?", "med", "Directed graph (digraph): edges have direction — u→v is not same as v→u. Used for: following (Twitter), dependencies (course prerequisites), web links. Undirected graph: edges are bidirectional — u—v is same as v—u. Used for: social networks (friend connections), road networks, communication networks. Directed: asymmetric relationships. Undirected: symmetric connections."],
  ["What is a weighted graph?", "med", "Weighted graph: edges have associated weights/costs. Used for: shortest path (Dijkstra's weights = distance), minimum spanning tree (weights = cost), network flow (weights = capacity), recommendation (weights = similarity). Adjacency list stores (neighbor, weight) pairs. Unweighted: all edges equal (BFS finds shortest path)."],
  ["What is a cycle in a graph?", "med", "Cycle: path starting and ending at same vertex with no repeated vertices (except first/last). Acyclic graph = no cycles. Detect cycles: DFS (back edge = edge to ancestor in DFS tree), Union-Find (for undirected), topological sort on DAG (if not possible → cycle). Uses: deadlock detection, dependency validation, recursion detection."],
  ["What is topological sorting?", "med", "Topological sort: linear ordering of DAG vertices where every edge u→v has u before v. Algorithms: Kahn's algorithm (BFS-based: compute indegrees, process 0-indegree vertices, decrement neighbors) O(V+E), DFS-based (postorder reverse) O(V+E). Only possible on DAGs. Uses: build systems (Make), course scheduling, dependency resolution, compilation order."],
  ["What is the sliding window technique?", "med", "Sliding window: maintain a subset of array elements that slides as you iterate. Fixed window: window of size k moves one step at a time. Variable (expanding/contracting): window grows/shrinks based on condition. Reduces O(n²) brute force to O(n). Pattern: left/right pointers, move right to expand, move left to shrink. Used for: maximum sum subarray, longest substring without repeats, substring with condition."],
  ["What is dynamic programming (DP)?", "med", "DP: technique for solving problems by breaking into overlapping subproblems, solving each once, storing results. Requirements: optimal substructure (optimal solution built from optimal sub-solutions), overlapping subproblems (same subproblems recur). Two approaches: top-down (recursion + memoization), bottom-up (tabulation — iterative, fill table). Classic DP: Fibonacci, knapSack, LCS, edit distance, coin change."],
  ["What is memoization vs tabulation?", "med", "Memoization (top-down): store results of expensive recursive calls. Cache result before returning. Recursive structure preserved. Only computes needed subproblems. May have recursion overhead. Tabulation (bottom-up): fill DP table iteratively from base cases up to target. No recursion overhead. Computes ALL subproblems (even unnecessary ones). Usually better performance (no recursion, no cache lookup overhead). Choose memoization for: natural recursion, sparse subproblems. Tabulation for: dense subproblems, performance."],
  ["What is the difference between greedy and DP?", "med", "Greedy: locally optimal choice at each step, hoping for global optimum. Simple, fast (O(n) or O(n log n)). May not find global optimum (counterexample: coin change with denominations 1,3,4 — greedy picks 4+1+1=3 coins, optimal is 3+3=2 coins). DP: explores all possibilities using subproblem results, guarantees global optimum. More complex. When greedy works: optimal substructure + greedy choice property (activity selection, Huffman coding, Dijkstra's)."],
  ["What is a deque (double-ended queue)?", "med", "Deque: queue where insertion/removal allowed at BOTH ends. Operations: addFront, addBack, removeFront, removeBack, peekFront, peekBack — all O(1) (amortized). Implemented with: doubly linked list or circular array (growing). Used in: sliding window maximum, palindrome checking, undo/redo (two-ended), task scheduling (priority at both ends). Can function as both stack and queue."],
  ["What is a circular queue?", "med", "Circular queue: fixed-size array where front/back wrap around using modulo. Empty: front == back. Full: (back+1) % size == front. Wastes one slot to distinguish empty/full (or use a count). Benefits: O(1) enqueue/dequeue, no shifting (unlike linear queue), memory efficient. Used in: CPU scheduling, IO buffers, streaming data processing. Fixed capacity — no dynamic resizing."],
  ["What is a monotonic stack?", "med", "Monotonic stack: stack that maintains elements in increasing or decreasing order. Pushing: pop while stack breaks monotonic property, then push. O(n) total — each element pushed/popped once. Used for: next greater element (NGE), previous greater element, largest rectangle in histogram, trapping rain water. Classic pattern: process elements left to right, use stack to maintain candidates for some condition."],
  ["What is prefix sum and when is it useful?", "med", "Prefix sum (cumulative sum): prefix[i] = sum(arr[0..i]). Precompute O(n), then range sum query: sum(l..r) = prefix[r] - prefix[l-1] in O(1). Used for: multiple range sum queries, subarray sum equals k, 2D prefix sum for matrix range queries, equalibrium index. Extension: prefix product, prefix XOR, difference array (for range updates)."],
  ["What is the difference between O(log n) and O(n log n)?", "med", "O(log n): logarithmic — time grows slowly as n increases (binary search, heap operations). n=1M → ~20 ops. O(n log n): linearithmic — linear times logarithmic (merge sort, quicksort average). n=1M → ~20M ops. O(log n) is faster (constant factor 1 vs n). O(n log n) is optimal for comparison-based sorting (proven lower bound)."],
  ["What is amortised time complexity?", "med", "Amortized complexity: average time per operation over a sequence of operations, even if individual operations may be expensive. Examples: dynamic array (vector) append — most O(1), but occasional resize O(n). Amortized: O(1) per append. Hash table resize — O(n) occasionally, amortized O(1) per insert. Amortized analysis: aggregate (total cost/n), accounting (prepay for expensive ops), potential (banker's view)."],

  // ── Hard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is Dijkstra's algorithm and its time complexity?", "hard", "Dijkstra's: shortest path from single source in weighted graph (non-negative edges). Maintains priority queue of distances, extracts min, relaxes neighbors. O((V+E) log V) with binary heap. O(E + V log V) with Fibonacci heap. Cannot handle negative edges (use Bellman-Ford for negative but no negative cycles). Greedy algorithm — guaranteed optimal because distances are non-decreasing."],
  ["What is the Bellman-Ford algorithm and when do you use it over Dijkstra's?", "hard", "Bellman-Ford: shortest path in weighted graph with NEGATIVE edges. O(VE) time. Relaxes ALL edges V-1 times. Can detect negative cycles (relax V-th iteration still changes distance). Use when: graph has negative edge weights, need to detect negative cycles. Slower than Dijkstra's (O(VE) vs O(E log V)) — only use when Dijkstra's doesn't apply."],
  ["What is Floyd-Warshall algorithm?", "hard", "Floyd-Warshall: ALL-pairs shortest path. O(V³) time, O(V²) space. DP approach: dp[i][j] = shortest path from i to j after considering vertices 0..k. Transition: dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][j], dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]). Works with negative edges (no negative cycles). Simpler than running Dijkstra V times (O(V(E log V))). Used for: transitive closure, finding diameter of graph, detecting negative cycles."],
  ["What is Kruskal's algorithm for Minimum Spanning Tree?", "hard", "Kruskal: MST by sorting edges by weight, adding edges that don't create cycles. O(E log E) or O(E log V) (sorting dominates). Uses Union-Find (DSU) for cycle detection. Algorithm: sort edges by weight, initialize DSU, for each edge (u,v,w): if find(u) ≠ find(v): union(u,v), add edge to MST. MST has V-1 edges. Good for sparse graphs."],
  ["What is Prim's algorithm for Minimum Spanning Tree?", "hard", "Prim: MST grows from a starting vertex by adding the cheapest edge connecting the tree to a new vertex. O((V+E) log V) with binary heap (same as Dijkstra's). Uses priority queue of (weight, vertex). For disconnected graphs: doesn't produce full MST (unlike Kruskal). Better for dense graphs (adjacency matrix: O(V²)). Both Prim and Kruskal produce correct MST (minimal sum of edge weights)."],
  ["What is the difference between MST and Shortest Path problems?", "hard", "MST: finds tree connecting ALL vertices with MINIMUM TOTAL EDGE WEIGHT. Single path between vertices, but not necessarily the shortest. Shortest Path: finds MINIMUM WEIGHT path between SPECIFIC vertices. Does not need to connect all vertices. MST minimizes sum; SP minimizes pair. MST uses all vertices (tree); SP uses a subset (path). Algorithms: Kruskal/Prim for MST; Dijkstra/Bellman-Ford for SP."],
  ["What is Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)?", "hard", "DSU: data structure tracking elements partitioned into disjoint sets. Operations: find(element) — returns set representative (O(α(n)) amortized). union(a,b) — merges sets containing a and b (O(α(n))). α(n) = inverse Ackermann function — practically constant. Optimizations: path compression (reparent all nodes in find to root), union by rank/size (attach smaller tree to larger). Used for: connected components, cycle detection, Kruskal's MST, percolation, image segmentation."],
  ["What is path compression and union by rank in DSU?", "hard", "Path compression: during find(), make each traversed node point directly to root. Collapses tree, keeping it flat. Union by rank: attach tree with smaller rank under larger rank's root. Rank ≈ height. Without optimization: O(n) worst-case find. With both: O(α(n)) amortized (practically O(1)). Size-based union (attach smaller to larger) also works well. Both optimizations are standard in DSU implementations."],
  ["What is Kosaraju's algorithm for Strongly Connected Components?", "hard", "Kosaraju: finds SCCs in directed graph. O(V+E). Steps: 1) DFS on original graph, store finish order, 2) Reverse graph direction, 3) DFS on reversed graph in decreasing finish order — each DFS tree = one SCC. Each SCC is a maximal set of vertices where every vertex can reach every other. Used for: detecting cycles in directed graphs, solving 2-SAT problems, dependency analysis."],
  ["What is Tarjan's algorithm for SCC?", "hard", "Tarjan: single DFS-based algorithm for SCCs. O(V+E). Uses low-link value: smallest discovery time reachable via subtree + one back edge. Maintains a stack of current DFS path. When discovery time == low-link: all nodes on stack until current form an SCC. More complex than Kosaraju but O(V+E) in one pass (no graph reversal). Used for: same applications as Kosaraju."],
  ["What is the Knapsack problem and its DP solution?", "hard", "0/1 Knapsack: given items with weight w[i] and value v[i], maximize total value with weight ≤ capacity W. Each item can be taken at most once. DP: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], dp[i-1][w-w[i]] + v[i]). Time O(nW), Space O(nW) (optimizable to O(W)). Unbounded Knapsack: each item unlimited. Variation: fractional knapsack (greedy works — take highest value/weight ratio). Classic NP-hard but O(nW) pseudo-polynomial."],
  ["What is the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) problem?", "hard", "LCS: find longest subsequence common to two strings (not necessarily contiguous). DP: dp[i][j] = LCS of s1[0..i-1] and s2[0..j-1]. Transition: if s1[i-1]==s2[j-1]: dp[i][j]=dp[i-1][j-1]+1; else dp[i][j]=max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]). Time O(mn), Space O(mn) (optimizable to O(min(m,n))). Used for: diff tools, bioinformatics (DNA sequence alignment), plagiarism detection."],
  ["What is the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)?", "hard", "LIS: find longest subsequence (not contiguous) that is strictly increasing. DP O(n²): dp[i] = 1 + max(dp[j]) for j<i and arr[j]<arr[i]. Optimal O(n log n): maintain tails[i] = smallest possible last element of LIS of length i+1. Binary search to find where current element fits. Used for: understanding sequence trends, patience sorting, data analysis."],
  ["What is the edit distance (Levenshtein) problem?", "hard", "Edit distance: minimum number of operations (insert, delete, replace) to transform string A to B. DP: dp[i][j] = min operations for A[0..i-1] → B[0..j-1]. Transition: if equal → dp[i-1][j-1]; else 1 + min(delete: dp[i-1][j], insert: dp[i][j-1], replace: dp[i-1][j-1]). Time O(mn), Space O(mn) (optimizable to O(min(m,n))). Used in: spell check, DNA sequence alignment, natural language processing."],
  ["What is the coin change problem?", "hard", "Coin change: minimum number of coins to make target amount (or count number of ways). Unbounded knapsack variant — unlimited coins. DP: dp[i] = minimum coins for amount i. Transition: dp[i] = min(dp[i-coin] + 1) for each coin ≤ i. Time O(amount × coins), Space O(amount). For counting ways: dp[i] += dp[i-coin]. Greedy (largest coin first) doesn't always work (denominations 1,3,4: amount 6 → greedy 4+1+1=3 vs optimal 3+3=2)."],
  ["What is the matrix chain multiplication problem?", "hard", "Matrix chain: find optimal parenthesization to minimize multiplications when multiplying matrices. DP: dp[i][j] = min operations for M[i..j]. Transition: dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + dims[i-1]*dims[k]*dims[j]) for k from i to j-1. Time O(n³), Space O(n²). Classic DP example showing how non-linear ordering of decisions requires DP over intervals."],
  ["What is a segment tree?", "hard", "Segment tree: tree storing interval information. Each node represents an array segment. Operations: build O(n), point update O(log n), range query O(log n) (sum, min, max, gcd, etc.). Space: O(4n) (array implementation). Recursive structure: parent covers [l,r], left child [l,mid], right child [mid+1,r]. Used for: range sum queries with updates, range minimum query with updates, interval scheduling. Lazy propagation enables range updates in O(log n)."],
  ["What is a Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree)?", "hard", "Fenwick tree (BIT): supports prefix sum queries and point updates in O(log n). Space: O(n). Simpler than segment tree (less code, less memory). Operations: update(index, delta): while i ≤ n: bit[i] += delta; i += i & -i. query(index): sum from 1 to i: while i > 0: sum += bit[i]; i -= i & -i. Range sum: query(r) - query(l-1). Cannot easily support range updates or non-invertible operations (min, max). Use segment tree for complex range queries."],
  ["What is a sparse table and when is it used?", "hard", "Sparse table: static data structure for RANGE QUERIES (no updates). Precompute st[i][k] = answer for range starting at i of length 2^k. Build O(n log n). Query O(1) for idempotent operations (min, max, gcd, lcm). Space O(n log n). For sum: O(log n) query (divide range into powers of 2). Not suitable for: dynamic data (updates require rebuild). Used for: RMQ (range minimum query) with no updates, LCA in trees (via Euler tour + RMQ)."],
  ["What is the difference between a segment tree and a Fenwick tree?", "hard", "Segment tree: O(4n) space, supports range queries AND range updates (with lazy propagation), works for any associative operation (sum, min, max, gcd), more complex. Fenwick tree: O(n) space, O(log n) point updates + prefix queries, only works for INVERTIBLE operations (sum, xor, not min/max), simpler code. Choose Fenwick for: prefix sums, point updates, simple queries. Choose Segment tree for: range updates, non-invertible ops, interval-based queries."],
  ["What is the KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) string matching algorithm?", "hard", "KMP: string matching in O(n+m) (n = text length, m = pattern length). Precomputes LPS (Longest Proper Prefix which is also Suffix) array for pattern in O(m). LPS[i] = longest border of pattern[0..i] (proper prefix = suffix, shorter than string). During matching: when mismatch at pattern[j], set j = LPS[j-1] (instead of restarting). Avoids redundant comparisons. Used for: substring search, DNA sequence matching, text editors (find)."],
  ["What is the Rabin-Karp algorithm?", "hard", "Rabin-Karp: string matching using rolling hash. Computes hash of pattern, slides window over text, compares hash. O(n+m) average, O(nm) worst-case (hash collisions). Rolling hash: hash = (prev_hash - char_out * base^(m-1)) * base + char_in. Uses modulo to handle large numbers. If hash matches: verify character by character (handles collisions). Used for: plagiarism detection (multiple pattern matching), substring search with multiple patterns."],
  ["What is Z-function in string matching?", "hard", "Z-function: Z[i] = longest common prefix of s and s[i..]. Computed in O(n) for string length n. Uses Z-box interval [l,r) — when i ≤ r, Z[i] = min(Z[i-l], r-i), then extend. Used for: pattern matching (concatenate pattern + '$' + text, compute Z — pattern found where Z[i] = |pattern|), string compression (Z[i] = n-i → period), palindrome detection. Alternative to KMP."],
  ["What is a suffix array?", "hard", "Suffix array: sorted list of all suffixes of a string (by lexicographic order). Length n. Build: O(n log n) (sorting) or O(n) (SA-IS algorithm). LCP array: longest common prefix between consecutive suffixes in suffix array. Used for: substring search (binary search on suffix array O(m log n)), longest repeated substring (max LCP), pattern matching, text compression (BWT → suffix array). Large memory (4n + 4n for SA + LCP)."],
  ["What is the Dutch National Flag problem?", "hard", "Dutch National Flag: sort array of 0s, 1s, 2s (three distinct values) in O(n) with O(1) space. Three pointers: low (next position for 0), mid (current element), high (next position for 2). While mid ≤ high: if arr[mid]==0: swap(arr[low], arr[mid]), low++, mid++; if arr[mid]==1: mid++; if arr[mid]==2: swap(arr[mid], arr[high]), high--. Partition into three sections. Variant of partition used in quicksort (3-way partition)."],
  ["What is a self-balancing BST and why is it important?", "hard", "Self-balancing BST: tree that automatically maintains height O(log n) after insertions and deletions. Examples: AVL, Red-Black, Splay, Treap. Importance: ensures worst-case O(log n) operations. Without balancing, BST degrades to O(n) linked list if inserts are ordered. Essential for database indexes, in-memory sorted data structures, associative containers (C++ map/set, Java TreeMap)."],
  ["What is a B-tree and where is it used?", "hard", "B-tree: self-balancing tree where nodes can have MULTIPLE children (not binary). Each node stores M keys, has M+1 children. All leaves at same depth. Order = maximum children per node (typical: hundreds, tuned to disk block size ~4KB). Search: O(log_M n) — very shallow depth. Used in: database indexes (MySQL, PostgreSQL), filesystems (NTFS, ext4, HFS+), key-value stores. Optimized for disk I/O (fewer seeks)."],
  ["What is the difference between a B-tree and B+ tree?", "hard", "B-tree: keys stored in ALL nodes (internal + leaves). Internal nodes have keys + children pointers. B+ tree: keys stored ONLY in LEAF nodes (or duplicate keys in internal nodes as separators). Internal nodes only contain routing keys. Leaves are linked (linked list for range scans). B+ tree advantages: range queries are O(log n + k) via leaf linked list, more keys fit in internal nodes (wider tree, shallower). InnoDB uses B+ tree."],
  ["What is a skip list?", "hard", "Skip list: layered linked list with express lanes. Each element has random height (coin flip tower). Search: start at highest level, move forward until next is larger, go down, repeat. O(log n) expected time. Pros: simple to implement, O(log n) operations, good concurrency. Cons: O(n) worst-case, random heights, more memory than balanced BST. Used in: Redis sorted sets (zset), LevelDB memtable, concurrent data structures."],
  ["What is consistent hashing and what problem does it solve?", "hard", "Consistent hashing: distributes keys across nodes in a ring (hash space 0..2^m-1). Each node maps to one or more positions on ring. Key assigned to first node clockwise. When a node is added/removed: only K/n keys remapped (K = total keys, n = nodes). Traditional modulo hashing: n changes → all keys remap (O(K) movement). Used in: CDNs (Akamai), distributed caches (Memcached, Redis Cluster), load balancers."],
  ["What is the difference between backtracking and dynamic programming?", "hard", "Backtracking: exhaustive search with pruning. Explores all possibilities, backtracks when path becomes invalid (N-Queens, Sudoku, subset sum). DP: optimal solution by solving overlapping subproblems. Backtracking explores decision TREE; DP explores DAG of subproblems. Backtracking + memoization = DP (top-down). Backtracking without memoization = exponential. Use DP when: optimal substructure + overlapping subproblems. Use backtracking when: exploring all solutions (not just optimal)."],
  ["What is a NP-complete problem? Give examples.", "hard", "NP-complete: problem whose solution is verifiable in polynomial time (NP), and any NP problem can be reduced to it (NP-hard). If any NP-complete problem can be solved in P time → P=NP (million dollar problem). Examples: SAT (boolean satisfiability), Traveling Salesman, 3-coloring, Subset Sum, Hamiltonian Cycle, Vertex Cover, KnapSack (decision version). No polynomial-time algorithm known — use approximation, heuristics, or exponential search."],
  ["What is the travelling salesman problem?", "hard", "TSP: find shortest possible route visiting each city exactly once and returning to start. NP-hard. Decision version: is there a route of length ≤ K? NP-complete. Solutions: DP (Held-Karp O(n²2ⁿ)), approximation (MST-based 2-approximation, Christofides 1.5-approximation), heuristics (nearest neighbor, 2-opt, simulated annealing, genetic algorithms). Practical for n ≤ 20 with exact DP, larger with heuristics."],
  ["What is the time and space complexity of common sorting algorithms table?", "hard", "Bubble: O(n²) time, O(1) space. Selection: O(n²), O(1). Insertion: O(n²), O(1). Quick: O(n log n) avg / O(n²) worst, O(log n). Merge: O(n log n), O(n). Heap: O(n log n), O(1). Counting: O(n+k), O(k). Radix: O(d(n+k)), O(n+k). Timsort (Python/Java): O(n log n), O(n). In practice: small n → insertion; general → quicksort (C qsort) or Timsort (Python sorted); memory-constrained → heap sort; stable needed → merge sort."],
  ["How do you find the kth largest element in an array efficiently?", "hard", "Quickselect (Hoare's selection): partition like quicksort, recurse on side containing kth. O(n) average, O(n²) worst. Optimized: random pivot, median-of-medians O(n) worst-case but high constant. Min-heap of size k: O(n log k) — insert elements, if heap size > k, pop smallest. Max-heap: O(n + k log n). For small k: heap approach is practical. For typical k (median): quickselect is fastest."],

  // ── New: Current/Industry Conceptual ──────────────────────────────────────
  ["When would you choose a hash map over a balanced BST?", "med", "Hash map: O(1) average lookups, no ordering. BST: O(log n) guaranteed, ordered traversal, range queries (find all keys between x and y), predecessor/successor. Choose hash map for: fast lookups, no ordering needed, simple key-value. Choose BST for: sorted iteration, range queries, order statistics (kth smallest), nearest key search."],
  ["When is an array preferable to a linked list for cache performance?", "med", "Arrays have cache locality — adjacent elements are in adjacent memory. CPUs load cache lines (64 bytes), so accessing arr[i] preloads arr[i+1..i+15]. Linked list nodes are scattered in memory — each traversal is a potential cache miss. Array: O(1) random access, O(n) insert/delete (shift). Linked list: O(n) access but O(1) insert/delete given node. For iteration-heavy workloads where access pattern is sequential, arrays are significantly faster."],
  ["Why does a heap give O(log n) insertion but O(1) peek?", "med", "Peek just returns root (arr[0]) — constant time. Insertion adds at end, then bubbles up (O(log n) swaps worst case). Extract min/max: replace root with last element, bubble down (O(log n)). Peek is trivially O(1) because the heap invariant ensures root is always min/max."],
  ["When should you use a trie instead of a hash map for string keys?", "med", "Trie advantages: prefix matching (autocomplete, longest prefix), O(m) search time (independent of n, number of keys), sorted iteration (DFS in lexicographic order). Hash map advantages: O(1) average lookup (vs O(m) for trie), less memory for many keys (no per-node child pointers). Use trie for: autocomplete, IP routing (longest prefix match), spell checker (prefix-based suggestions), dictionary with prefixes."],
  ["What problem class does the sliding window pattern solve well?", "med", "Sliding window solves subarray/substring problems with CONTIGUOUS elements. Common types: fixed window (maximum sum of k consecutive elements), variable window with constraint (longest substring without repeating characters), two pointers with condition (minimum window containing all target characters). Converts O(n²) brute force to O(n). Pattern: maintain window [left, right], expand right, contract left when constraint violated."],
  ["When is the two-pointer technique applicable to a sorted array?", "easy", "Two pointers (left/right from ends) works on SORTED arrays for: pair sum equals target (adjust based on sum vs target), three sum (fix one, two-pointer on rest), container with most water (move shorter pointer), removing duplicates (slow/fast pointer). Beyond sorted: fast/slow pointers for cycle detection in linked lists."],
  ["Why is BFS preferred over DFS for shortest path in an unweighted graph?", "med", "BFS explores level by level — the first time a node is reached is ALWAYS via the SHORTEST path (fewest edges). DFS explores depth-first — the first time a node is reached may NOT be the shortest path. BFS guarantees shortest path in O(V+E). For weighted graphs, Dijkstra's is needed (BFS doesn't account for edge weights)."],
  ["When would you pick DFS over BFS for a graph problem?", "med", "DFS for: path existence (does path exist between nodes?), topological sort, cycle detection, connected components, articulation points/bridges, solving puzzles that require exploring complete paths. BFS for: shortest path (unweighted), closer neighbors first, web crawling (same-level pages), finding connected components in infinite graphs (BFS finds neighbors before going deep). DFS uses less memory on deep graphs (stores path, not whole frontier)."],
  ["What signals in a problem statement hint at a dynamic programming solution?", "med", "Keywords: 'maximum/minimum', 'number of ways', 'optimal', 'subarray/subsequence', 'can you partition?', 'shortest/longest'. Characteristics: optimal substructure (optimal solution built from subproblems), overlapping subproblems (repeating recusive calls), decisions affecting future options (you can't greedily decide), constraints (e.g., knapSack capacity, string lengths ≤ n). Ask: 'Can I solve this by trying all possibilities and storing results?'"],
  ["When is memoization preferable to tabulation in practice?", "med", "Memoization: only computes needed subproblems, natural recursive structure, easier to implement from recurrence. Use when: subproblem graph is sparse (not all subproblems needed), natural recursion exists, problem state space is large but only some states reachable. Tabulation: no recursion overhead, better performance (no cache lookups), often uses less memory. Use when: all subproblems are needed (LCS, Floyd-Warshall), or DP table structure is simple."],
  ["Why does quicksort outperform mergesort in practice despite same average complexity?", "hard", "Quicksort: better cache locality (in-place partitioning scans linearly through array). Mergesort: O(n) extra space (temporary arrays), more memory accesses, cache-unfriendly merge step. Quicksort's constant factors are lower (about 2-3x faster). Quicksort's worst-case (O(n²)) is rare with good pivot selection (random, median-of-three). Mergesort is stable and has guaranteed O(n log n) — chosen for: libraries needing stable sort (Python, Java for objects)."],
  ["When would you choose mergesort over quicksort?", "med", "Mergesort over quicksort: 1) Stability required (maintain relative order of equal elements), 2) Linked lists (no extra space needed for list merge), 3) External sorting (data too large for memory — merge sort works on disk), 4) Worst-case guarantees needed (O(n log n) regardless of input), 5) Parallel computing (merge sort parallelizes well). Unsorted arrays in memory with stability needed? Mergesort."],
  ["Why is a min-heap the natural structure for a streaming top-k problem?", "med", "Min-heap of size k: for each element, push, if heap size > k, pop smallest. After all elements, heap contains k largest elements. O(n log k) time, O(k) space. A max-heap would need O(k + n log k) with unnecessary popping. A min-heap keeps track of the 'threshold' to enter top-k — larger elements replace the smallest in the heap."],
  ["When is a monotonic stack the right tool for an array problem?", "med", "Monotonic stack for problems involving: next greater element (NGE), previous greater element, nearest smaller/greater, largest rectangle in histogram (classic), trapping rainwater, sliding window maximum (though deque is better). Pattern: for each element, array is processed left-to-right, stack maintains potential candidates, popping when element breaks monotonic property."],
  ["How do you choose a data structure for an LRU cache and why?", "hard", "LRU cache: O(1) get and put. Combination: Hash map (key → node pointer) + Doubly linked list (head = least recently used, tail = most recent). Get: if key exists in map, move node to tail (delete from current position, add to tail), return value. Put: if key exists, update value, move to tail. If full: remove head node, delete from map, add new node at tail. Doubly linked list allows O(1) node removal and insertion."],
  ["When is Union-Find better than DFS for connectivity queries?", "hard", "Union-Find: ideal for DYNAMIC connectivity (edges added over time). Union operations O(α(n)), find queries O(α(n)). DFS: requires O(V+E) per query (rebuild). Use Union-Find when: edges are added incrementally, many connectivity queries on changing graph, Kruskal's MST, offline percolation problems. Use DFS for: static graph, need path info (not just connectivity), SCC decomposition."],
  ["Why does Dijkstra's fail on graphs with negative edges?", "hard", "Dijkstra's assumes that once a node is processed (extracted from PQ), its shortest distance is final (greedy). With negative edges, a later node could find a shorter path to an already-processed node. The algorithm doesn't revisit processed nodes. Example: s→a (10), s→b (1), b→a (-5). Dijkstra processes b first (dist=1), then a via s (dist=10) or a via b (dist=1+(-5)=-4) — but b is already processed. Bellman-Ford handles negatives by iterating V-1 times."],
  ["When would you reach for a segment tree over a prefix sum array?", "hard", "Segment tree: supports updates + queries. Prefix sum: O(1) range sum but O(n) updates. Use prefix sum when: array is STATIC (no updates), only sum queries needed. Use segment tree when: array changes frequently, need non-sum operations (min, max, gcd), need range updates (add value to range) with lazy propagation. BIT is a compromise: O(log n) updates + prefix queries, simpler than segment tree."],
  ["What makes consistent hashing suitable for distributed caches?", "hard", "Consistent hashing minimizes key redistribution when nodes change. Adding/removing a node: only K/n keys need to move (K = total keys, n = nodes). Modulo hashing: all keys remap (complete cache miss storm). Virtual nodes (multiple ring positions per physical node) improve load balancing. Used in: Memcached distributed cache, Redis Cluster, Amazon Dynamo, CDNs. Supports hot spot mitigation and weighted nodes."],
  ["When is a B+ tree the right choice for a database index?", "hard", "B+ tree for: range queries (leaf linked list enables O(log n + k) scans), large datasets (shallow tree, 3-4 levels for millions), disk I/O optimization (node size = disk block, few seeks per query), insert-heavy workloads (high fanout minimizes rebalancing). Hash index for: exact-match lookups (O(1)), no ordering needed, low-memory scenarios. B+ tree is the default for most relational databases (MySQL InnoDB, PostgreSQL)."],
  ["Why are tries used in autocomplete and IP routing systems?", "med", "Tries enable: prefix-based search in O(m) (m = query length, independent of total entries). Autocomplete: traverse by prefix, then DFS/BFS for suggestions. IP routing (Patricia trie/radix tree): longest prefix match for routing table lookup. Memory efficient variants (compressed trie, radix tree, ternary search tree) reduce child pointer overhead. Hash map: O(1) exact lookup but cannot answer 'what keys start with prefix X?'."],
  ["When does counting sort beat comparison-based sorts?", "med", "Counting sort: O(n+k) time, O(k) space (k = range of values). Beats O(n log n) comparison sorts when k = O(n). Example: sort 1M students by age (0-100) — O(n+k) where k=100 is much faster than O(n log n). Constraint: values must be integers (or map to integer), known range, memory O(k) acceptable. Radix sort extends this to larger k by sorting digit by digit."],
  ["How do you decide between recursion and an explicit stack?", "med", "Recursion: simpler, more readable, natural for tree/graph traversals and divide-and-conquer. Risks: stack overflow for deep recursion (default ~1KB per frame, limit ~8MB), no control over stack size. Explicit stack: avoids stack overflow, can use heap memory, more control, harder to read. Use recursion for: balanced trees (max depth ~log n), small problems, simplicity. Use explicit stack for: deep graphs (potentially O(n) depth), iterative solutions needed."],
  ["When is amortized analysis the right way to reason about a structure?", "med", "Amortized analysis when: individual operations may be expensive but average cost over sequence is bounded. Examples: dynamic array (vector) push_back — O(n) resize occasionally, O(1) amortized. Hash table insert — occasional O(n) resize, O(1) amortized. Binary counter increment — O(log n) worst, O(1) amortized. Use amortized for: overall performance of a series of operations matters, not individual worst-case."],
  ["Why is a balanced BST chosen over a hash map for ordered range queries?", "med", "Balanced BST: in-order traversal gives sorted order O(n), range query (find all keys between x and y) O(log n + k). Hash map: no ordering, can't do range queries or find predecessor/successor. BST also provides: floor/ceiling (nearest key), kth smallest, rank of a key. When order matters: use BST (or B-tree for disk-based). When only exact lookups: hash map is faster."],
  ["When would you use a bloom filter and what trade-off does it make?", "hard", "Bloom filter: probabilistic set membership. O(k) hash computations per query, O(m) space (m = bit array size). False positives possible (says 'yes' when not present), false negatives impossible. Trade-off: space efficiency vs. small error rate. Optimal: m = -n ln p / (ln 2)², k = m/n ln 2. Use for: cache filtering (avoid cache misses on non-existent keys?), web crawler URL deduplication, spell checker (not in dictionary?), Medium 'recommendations you've seen'."],
  ["Why does binary search require a monotonic predicate, not just sorted data?", "med", "Binary search requires a predicate that is true for some prefix and false for the rest (or vice versa). Sorted data provides this: arr[i] < target is false for prefix, true for suffix (or false for prefix, true for suffix). But binary search can be applied to: find first true in boolean array, find peak in mountain array (bitonic), find rotation point in rotated sorted array. Key insight: it's not about sorted data — it's about finding the boundary where a condition changes."],
  ["When is bucket sort effective and what assumption does it rely on?", "med", "Bucket sort: distribute elements into buckets, sort each bucket, concatenate. Effective when: data is UNIFORMLY DISTRIBUTED over a known range. O(n + k) average, O(n²) worst (all in one bucket). Assumption: uniform distribution (preschool GPA, not 99th percentile test scores). Best for: floating point numbers in [0,1), uniformly distributed keys. Each bucket sort typically uses insertion sort (small size)."],
  ["How do you pick a graph representation for a sparse vs dense graph?", "med", "Sparse (E ≈ V): adjacency list — O(V+E) space, iterate neighbors fast (degree). Good for: social networks, web graphs, road networks. Dense (E ≈ V²): adjacency matrix — O(V²) space, O(1) edge lookup. Good for: Floyd-Warshall, small graphs. Most real-world graphs are sparse — adjacency list is the default. Edge list: list of (u,v,w) triples — compact for algorithms that iterate edges (Kruskal), not good for neighbor lookups."],
  ["When is backtracking preferable to brute-force enumeration?", "med", "Backtracking: explores search tree with PRUNING — skips branches that cannot lead to valid solution. Brute force: generates ALL possibilities, then filters. Backtracking vs brute force: both O(choices^depth) worst-case without pruning. But backtracking often prunes heavily (constraint satisfaction, N-Queens). Use backtracking when: constraints can be checked incrementally, partial solutions can be invalid (prune early). Use brute force when: constraints can only be checked on complete solutions."],
  ["Why does a Fenwick tree use less memory than a segment tree?", "hard", "Fenwick tree: O(n) memory — exactly n+1 sized array. Segment tree: O(4n) memory (array implementation to ensure complete binary tree). BIT's structure is more memory-efficient because it only tracks prefix versions of the data. Segment tree needs extra space for internal nodes. For n=1M: BIT = 4MB, Segment tree = 16MB (assuming 4-byte integers)."],
  ["When would you shard a hash table across machines?", "hard", "Shard (distribute) hash table when: single machine memory insufficient (data > 100GB), throughput exceeds single node capacity, fault tolerance needed (replicas). Approaches: consistent hashing (minimal remapping on node change), modulo (simpler but bad for scaling), fixed partition. Challenges: cross-shard queries, transactions, rebalancing, hot spots. Real-world: Redis Cluster, Amazon DynamoDB, Apache Cassandra."],
  ["How do you choose a heap vs sorting for a k-largest query on a stream?", "med", "Stream (infinite data, unknown total): min-heap of size k — O(n log k), O(k) space. Only processes each element once. Sorting: requires storing ALL elements, O(n log n) time, O(n) space. For streaming data (infinite, cannot store all): heap is the only option. For small k relative to n: heap is better (O(n log k) vs O(n log n)). For k close to n (e.g., median): quickselect or sorting may be better."],
  ["When is topological sort the correct framing for a dependency problem?", "med", "Topological sort for: build systems (must compile module A before B), course prerequisites (must take CS101 before CS201), task scheduling with dependencies, package managers (install dependencies first), data pipeline ordering, deadlock detection (cycle in dependency graph). If problem involves 'depends on', 'must precede', 'prerequisite' — topological sort on DAG is likely the solution."],
  ["Why is a doubly linked list needed in an O(1) LRU cache?", "med", "LRU cache: when accessed, move to front. When full, remove from back. Singly linked list: O(1) remove given node? No — need previous node. Doubly linked list: O(1) remove node (prev/next pointers). For move-to-front: remove node (O(1) via DDL), add to front (O(1)). Hash map provides O(1) node lookup. Combination: hash map (pointer to DDL node) + DDL (order tracking) = O(1) LRU cache."],

  // ── New: Code / Implementation ────────────────────────────────────────────
  [`Write an algorithm to reverse a singly linked list in place.`, "med", "function reverseList(head) { let prev = null; let curr = head; while (curr) { let nextTemp = curr.next; // save next curr.next = prev; // reverse prev = curr; // advance prev curr = nextTemp; // advance curr } return prev; // new head }. Time O(n), Space O(1). Recursive: if (!head || !head.next) return head; let newHead = reverseList(head.next); head.next.next = head; head.next = null; return newHead."],
  [`Implement binary search on a sorted array.`, "easy", "function binarySearch(arr, target) { let left = 0, right = arr.length - 1; while (left <= right) { let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2); if (arr[mid] === target) return mid; if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1; else right = mid - 1; } return -1; }. Time O(log n), Space O(1). Edge cases: empty array, target at ends, duplicates."],
  [`Write an algorithm to detect a cycle in a linked list.`, "med", "function hasCycle(head) { let slow = head, fast = head; while (fast && fast.next) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; if (slow === fast) return true; } return false; // no cycle }. Floyd's Tortoise and Hare — O(n) time, O(1) space. To find cycle start: after detecting cycle, move slow to head, advance both one step until they meet again — that's cycle start."],
  [`Implement BFS traversal of a graph from a source node.`, "med", "function bfs(graph, start) { const visited = new Set(); const queue = [start]; visited.add(start); while (queue.length) { const node = queue.shift(); console.log(node); for (const neighbor of graph[node]) { if (!visited.has(neighbor)) { visited.add(neighbor); queue.push(neighbor); } } } }. Time O(V+E), Space O(V). Use array as queue or proper Queue class for performance."],
  [`Implement DFS traversal of a graph recursively.`, "med", "function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set()) { if (visited.has(node)) return; console.log(node); visited.add(node); for (const neighbor of graph[node]) { dfs(graph, neighbor, visited); } }. Time O(V+E), Space O(V) (recursion stack). Can also use explicit stack: stack = [start]; while (stack.length) { node = stack.pop(); if (!visited.has(node)) { visited.add(node); for (neighbor of graph[node]) stack.push(neighbor); } }."],
  [`Two Sum: return indices of two numbers that add up to a target.`, "easy", "function twoSum(nums, target) { const map = new Map(); // value → index for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) { const complement = target - nums[i]; if (map.has(complement)) return [map.get(complement), i]; map.set(nums[i], i); } return []; }. O(n) time, O(n) space. One pass: store seen numbers, check complement."],
  [`Valid Parentheses: check if a string of brackets is balanced.`, "easy", "function isValid(s) { const stack = []; const pairs = { '(': ')', '{': '}', '[': ']' }; for (const char of s) { if (char in pairs) stack.push(pairs[char]); else if (stack.pop() !== char) return false; } return stack.length === 0; }. O(n) time, O(n) space. Stack tracks expected closing brackets."],
  [`Merge two sorted linked lists into one sorted list.`, "easy", "function mergeTwoLists(l1, l2) { const dummy = new ListNode(); let curr = dummy; while (l1 && l2) { if (l1.val <= l2.val) { curr.next = l1; l1 = l1.next; } else { curr.next = l2; l2 = l2.next; } curr = curr.next; } curr.next = l1 || l2; return dummy.next; }. O(n+m) time, O(1) space."],
  [`Maximum Subarray: find the contiguous subarray with the largest sum.`, "med", "function maxSubArray(nums) { let maxSoFar = nums[0], maxEndingHere = nums[0]; for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) { maxEndingHere = Math.max(nums[i], maxEndingHere + nums[i]); maxSoFar = Math.max(maxSoFar, maxEndingHere); } return maxSoFar; }. Kadane's algorithm: O(n) time, O(1) space. Dynamic programming: dp[i] = max subarray ending at i."],
  [`Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock: find max profit from one transaction.`, "easy", "function maxProfit(prices) { let minPrice = Infinity; let maxProfit = 0; for (const price of prices) { minPrice = Math.min(minPrice, price); maxProfit = Math.max(maxProfit, price - minPrice); } return maxProfit; }. O(n) time, O(1) space. Track minimum seen so far, compute profit at each step."],
  [`Implement quicksort with a partition function.`, "med", "function quickSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) { if (left >= right) return; const pivotIndex = partition(arr, left, right); quickSort(arr, left, pivotIndex - 1); quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, right); return arr; } function partition(arr, left, right) { const pivot = arr[right]; let i = left; for (let j = left; j < right; j++) { if (arr[j] < pivot) { [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]]; i++; } } [arr[i], arr[right]] = [arr[right], arr[i]]; return i; }. Time O(n log n) avg, O(n²) worst. Space O(log n)."],
  [`Implement mergesort on an integer array.`, "med", "function mergeSort(arr) { if (arr.length <= 1) return arr; const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2); const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid)); const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid)); return merge(left, right); } function merge(left, right) { const result = []; let i = 0, j = 0; while (i < left.length && j < right.length) { result.push(left[i] <= right[j] ? left[i++] : right[j++]); } return [...result, ...left.slice(i), ...right.slice(j)]; }. Time O(n log n), Space O(n)."],
  [`Find the middle node of a linked list in one pass.`, "easy", "function middleNode(head) { let slow = head, fast = head; while (fast && fast.next) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; } return slow; }. Floyd's Tortoise and Hare: slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps. When fast reaches end, slow is at middle. O(n) time, O(1) space."],
  [`Climbing Stairs: count ways to reach step n taking 1 or 2 steps.`, "easy", "function climbStairs(n) { if (n <= 2) return n; let prev1 = 1, prev2 = 2; for (let i = 3; i <= n; i++) { const curr = prev1 + prev2; prev1 = prev2; prev2 = curr; } return prev2; }. DP: dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] (Fibonacci). O(n) time, O(1) space. O(1) space using two variables instead of full DP array."],
  [`Implement an LRU cache with O(1) get and put.`, "hard", "class LRUCache { constructor(capacity) { this.cap = capacity; this.cache = new Map(); } get(key) { if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1; const val = this.cache.get(key); this.cache.delete(key); this.cache.set(key, val); // move to end (most recent) return val; } put(key, value) { if (this.cache.has(key)) this.cache.delete(key); this.cache.set(key, value); if (this.cache.size > this.cap) { const lruKey = this.cache.keys().next().value; // first key = LRU this.cache.delete(lruKey); } } }. JS Map preserves insertion order — ideal for LRU. O(1) get/put."],
  [`Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters using a sliding window.`, "med", "function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) { const charMap = new Map(); let maxLen = 0, left = 0; for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) { const char = s[right]; if (charMap.has(char) && charMap.get(char) >= left) { left = charMap.get(char) + 1; } charMap.set(char, right); maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1); } return maxLen; }. O(n) time, O(min(m, n)) space (m = charset size)."],
  [`Number of Islands: count connected land regions in a grid.`, "med", "function numIslands(grid) { let count = 0; for (let r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) { for (let c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++) { if (grid[r][c] === '1') { count++; dfs(grid, r, c); } } } return count; } function dfs(grid, r, c) { if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] !== '1') return; grid[r][c] = '0'; // mark visited dfs(grid, r-1, c); dfs(grid, r+1, c); dfs(grid, r, c-1); dfs(grid, r, c+1); }. O(mn) time, O(mn) worst-space (skewed islands)."],
  [`Implement level-order traversal of a binary tree.`, "med", "function levelOrder(root) { if (!root) return []; const result = [], queue = [root]; while (queue.length) { const levelSize = queue.length; const level = []; for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) { const node = queue.shift(); level.push(node.val); if (node.left) queue.push(node.left); if (node.right) queue.push(node.right); } result.push(level); } return result; }. BFS using queue. O(n) time, O(n) space."],
  [`Invert a binary tree.`, "easy", "function invertTree(root) { if (!root) return null; const temp = root.left; root.left = invertTree(root.right); root.right = invertTree(temp); return root; }. O(n) time, O(h) space (h = height). Swap children recursively. Iterative: stack/queue with swap."],
  [`Validate whether a binary tree is a valid BST.`, "med", "function isValidBST(root, min = -Infinity, max = Infinity) { if (!root) return true; if (root.val <= min || root.val >= max) return false; return isValidBST(root.left, min, root.val) && isValidBST(root.right, root.val, max); }. O(n) time, O(h) space. In-order traversal alternative: check that in-order list is strictly increasing."],
  [`Lowest Common Ancestor of two nodes in a BST.`, "med", "function lowestCommonAncestor(root, p, q) { while (root) { if (p.val < root.val && q.val < root.val) root = root.left; else if (p.val > root.val && q.val > root.val) root = root.right; else return root; } return null; }. O(h) time, O(1) space. BST property: LCA is node where p and q diverge (one on left, one on right)."],
  [`Implement Dijkstra's shortest path using a priority queue.`, "hard", "function dijkstra(graph, start) { const dist = {}; const pq = new MinPriorityQueue(); for (const v in graph) dist[v] = Infinity; dist[start] = 0; pq.enqueue(start, 0); while (!pq.isEmpty()) { const { element: u } = pq.dequeue(); for (const { node: v, weight: w } of graph[u]) { if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) { dist[v] = dist[u] + w; pq.enqueue(v, dist[v]); } } } return dist; }. O((V+E) log V) time, O(V) space."],
  [`Coin Change: minimum coins to make a target amount with DP.`, "med", "function coinChange(coins, amount) { const dp = new Array(amount + 1).fill(Infinity); dp[0] = 0; for (let i = 1; i <= amount; i++) { for (const coin of coins) { if (i >= coin) dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - coin] + 1); } } return dp[amount] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[amount]; }. Time O(amount × coins), Space O(amount)."],
  [`Longest Common Subsequence of two strings with DP.`, "hard", "function longestCommonSubsequence(text1, text2) { const m = text1.length, n = text2.length; const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0)); for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) { for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) { if (text1[i-1] === text2[j-1]) dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1; else dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]); } } return dp[m][n]; }. O(mn) time, O(mn) space (can optimize to O(min(m,n)))."],
  [`Implement a trie with insert, search, and startsWith.`, "med", "class TrieNode { constructor() { this.children = {}; this.isEnd = false; } } class Trie { constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); } insert(word) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { if (!node.children[ch]) node.children[ch] = new TrieNode(); node = node.children[ch]; } node.isEnd = true; } search(word) { let node = this._traverse(word); return node !== null && node.isEnd; } startsWith(prefix) { return this._traverse(prefix) !== null; } _traverse(str) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of str) { if (!node.children[ch]) return null; node = node.children[ch]; } return node; } }. O(m) per operation."],
  [`Find the kth largest element using a min-heap of size k.`, "med", "function findKthLargest(nums, k) { const minHeap = []; for (const num of nums) { minHeap.push(num); if (minHeap.length > k) { minHeap.sort((a,b) => a-b); minHeap.shift(); // remove smallest } } return minHeap[0]; }. Without built-in heap: use MinPriorityQueue. O(n log k) time, O(k) space. Quickselect alternative: O(n) average."],
  [`Product of Array Except Self without using division.`, "med", "function productExceptSelf(nums) { const n = nums.length; const result = new Array(n).fill(1); // left products let left = 1; for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { result[i] = left; left *= nums[i]; } // right products let right = 1; for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) { result[i] *= right; right *= nums[i]; } return result; }. O(n) time, O(1) space (excluding output)."],
  [`Implement quickselect to find the kth smallest element.`, "hard", "function quickSelect(arr, k) { return select(arr, 0, arr.length - 1, k - 1); } function select(arr, left, right, k) { if (left === right) return arr[left]; const pivotIndex = partition(arr, left, right); if (k === pivotIndex) return arr[k]; if (k < pivotIndex) return select(arr, left, pivotIndex - 1, k); return select(arr, pivotIndex + 1, right, k); }. O(n) average, O(n²) worst. Partition is same as quicksort."],
  [`Group Anagrams from a list of strings.`, "med", "function groupAnagrams(strs) { const map = new Map(); for (const str of strs) { const key = str.split('').sort().join(''); if (!map.has(key)) map.set(key, []); map.get(key).push(str); } return Array.from(map.values()); }. O(n × k log k) time (k = max string length), O(nk) space. Optimization: use character count as key: '1a0b2c...'. Same complexity but O(nk) time."],
  [`Implement topological sort using Kahn's algorithm.`, "hard", "function topologicalSort(numNodes, edges) { const inDegree = new Array(numNodes).fill(0); const adj = Array.from({ length: numNodes }, () => []); for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); inDegree[v]++; } const queue = []; for (let i = 0; i < numNodes; i++) { if (inDegree[i] === 0) queue.push(i); } const result = []; while (queue.length) { const node = queue.shift(); result.push(node); for (const neighbor of adj[node]) { inDegree[neighbor]--; if (inDegree[neighbor] === 0) queue.push(neighbor); } } return result.length === numNodes ? result : []; // empty = cycle }. O(V+E) time, O(V) space."],
  [`Word Ladder: shortest transformation sequence length using BFS.`, "hard", "function ladderLength(beginWord, endWord, wordList) { const wordSet = new Set(wordList); if (!wordSet.has(endWord)) return 0; let queue = [beginWord], level = 1; while (queue.length) { const nextQueue = []; for (const word of queue) { if (word === endWord) return level; for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) { for (let ch = 97; ch <= 122; ch++) { const newWord = word.slice(0,i) + String.fromCharCode(ch) + word.slice(i+1); if (wordSet.has(newWord)) { wordSet.delete(newWord); nextQueue.push(newWord); } } } } queue = nextQueue; level++; } return 0; }. O(n × m × 26) time (n=words, m=word length). BFS for shortest path."],
  [`Implement an algorithm to check if a string is a palindrome ignoring case.`, "easy", "function isPalindrome(s) { const cleaned = s.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '').toLowerCase(); let left = 0, right = cleaned.length - 1; while (left < right) { if (cleaned[left] !== cleaned[right]) return false; left++; right--; } return true; }. O(n) time, O(1) space (or O(n) for cleaned string). Two-pointer approach."],
  [`Rotate an array to the right by k steps in place.`, "med", "function rotate(nums, k) { k = k % nums.length; reverse(nums, 0, nums.length - 1); reverse(nums, 0, k - 1); reverse(nums, k, nums.length - 1); } function reverse(arr, left, right) { while (left < right) { [arr[left], arr[right]] = [arr[right], arr[left]]; left++; right--; } }. O(n) time, O(1) space. Three reversals: full, first k, remaining n-k."],
  [`Implement a min-stack supporting getMin in O(1).`, "med", "class MinStack { constructor() { this.stack = []; this.minStack = []; } push(val) { this.stack.push(val); if (this.minStack.length === 0 || val <= this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) { this.minStack.push(val); } } pop() { const val = this.stack.pop(); if (val === this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) this.minStack.pop(); return val; } top() { return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1]; } getMin() { return this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]; } }. O(1) for all operations. Space O(n) for minStack."],
  [`Edit Distance between two strings using dynamic programming.`, "hard", "function minDistance(word1, word2) { const m = word1.length, n = word2.length; const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0)); for (let i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i; for (let j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j; for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) { for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) { if (word1[i-1] === word2[j-1]) dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]; else dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min( dp[i-1][j], // delete dp[i][j-1], // insert dp[i-1][j-1] // replace ); } } return dp[m][n]; }. O(mn) time, O(mn) space."],

  // ── New: Tricky Output / Complexity ───────────────────────────────────────
  [`What is the time complexity of two nested loops over n elements?`, "easy", "O(n²). Outer loop runs n times, inner runs n times — total operations ≈ n × n = n². Example: for i in range(n): for j in range(n): constant_work(). If inner loop runs fewer iterations (j < i), it's O(n²/2) = O(n²) still."],
  [`What is the time complexity of binary search on a sorted array of size n?`, "easy", "O(log n). Each iteration halves the search space: n → n/2 → n/4 → ... → 1. Number of iterations = log₂(n). Binary search on 1M elements takes ~20 comparisons."],
  [`What is the time complexity of building a heap from n elements?`, "med", "O(n) — this is counterintuitive. Heapify (building heap from array) runs in O(n) using Floyd's down-heap method. Proof: sum over all nodes of (h × number of nodes at height h). The sum converges to O(n). Per-operation insert is O(log n), but constructing from scratch is O(n)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of mergesort and why?`, "med", "O(n log n). Divide: each level splits array into halves (log n levels). Merge: each level merges n elements total (O(n) per level). Total = n × log n operations. Same for best, worst, average cases."],
  [`What is the worst-case time complexity of quicksort and when does it occur?`, "med", "O(n²). Occurs when partitions are highly unbalanced (pivot is always min or max). This happens when: array is already sorted and pivot is first/last element, or array has all equal elements. Avoid with: random pivot, median-of-three pivot selection, or introsort (hybrid with heap sort for recursion depth > log n)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of inserting into a balanced BST?`, "easy", "O(log n) — search for correct insertion point (height of tree) and insert. Balanced BST (AVL, Red-Black) maintains height = O(log n). Unbalanced BST worst-case: O(n)."],
  [`What is the amortized time complexity of appending to a dynamic array?`, "med", "O(1) amortized. Most appends are O(1). When array is full, resize (double capacity): O(n) to copy elements. Over n inserts: total copy cost = O(n) (1 + 2 + 4 + ... + n ≈ 2n). Average per operation = O(1)."],
  [`What is the space complexity of recursive depth-first search on a tree?`, "med", "O(h) where h is the tree height. Each recursive call adds a stack frame. For balanced tree: O(log n). For skewed tree: O(n). Stack overflow risk for deep trees. Iterative DFS with explicit stack uses same O(h) space but heap-allocated (less overflow risk)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of BFS on a graph with V vertices and E edges?`, "med", "O(V + E). Each vertex is enqueued once (O(V)), each edge is traversed once (from each vertex, iterate adjacency list — O(E)). For dense graphs (E ≈ V²), this is O(V²). For sparse (E ≈ V), O(V)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of Dijkstra's with a binary heap?`, "hard", "O((V+E) log V). Each vertex extracted from heap (V × O(log V)), each edge relaxes (E × O(log V) — decrease-key). With Fibonacci heap: O(E + V log V). With array (no heap): O(V²) — better for dense graphs."],
  [`What does a preorder traversal output for a BST built from 5,3,7,2,4?`, "med", "BST structure: root=5, left=3 (left=2, right=4), right=7. Preorder (root → left → right): 5, 3, 2, 4, 7. Inorder (sorted): 2, 3, 4, 5, 7. Postorder: 2, 4, 3, 7, 5."],
  [`What is the time complexity of a hash map lookup in the worst case?`, "med", "O(n) — when all keys hash to the same bucket (collision chain). In practice with good hash function and load factor < 0.75: O(1) average. Worst case is rare but possible. Tree-based collision (Java 8+) converts long chains to balanced trees for O(log n) worst case."],
  [`What is the time complexity of nested loops where the inner runs i times?`, "med", "O(n²). For i from 0 to n: for j from 0 to i: constant_work(). Total = 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n = n(n+1)/2 = O(n²). The constant factor (1/2) is dropped in Big-O."],
  [`What is the time complexity of a recursive Fibonacci without memoization?`, "med", "O(2ⁿ) exponential. Recursion tree: each call spawns 2 calls, depth = n. Total calls ≈ 2ⁿ. T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2) + 1 ≈ O(2ⁿ). This is why naive Fibonacci is intractable for n > 50."],
  [`What is the time complexity of the same Fibonacci with memoization?`, "med", "O(n) — each n computed once. With memoization: T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2) but each value computed once. Total = O(n). Space: O(n) for cache + O(n) recursion stack (or O(1) with iterative tabulation). The difference is exponential vs linear."],
  [`What does for i in n: for j in i..n cost in Big O?`, "med", "O(n²). Outer loop: n iterations. Inner loop: n-i+1 iterations per outer iteration. Total ≈ n + (n-1) + ... + 1 = n(n+1)/2 = O(n²). Despite inner loop shrinking, it's still quadratic."],
  [`What is the output of an inorder traversal of a BST?`, "easy", "Sorted order (ascending). BST property: left < root < right ensures inorder (left, root, right) visits elements in increasing order. For BST with values [2,3,4,5,7]: inorder = 2, 3, 4, 5, 7. Reverse inorder (right, root, left) gives descending order."],
  [`What is the time complexity of searching an unbalanced BST in the worst case?`, "med", "O(n) — degenerate BST that is essentially a linked list (each node has only one child). This happens when inserting sorted data without rebalancing. Balanced BST guarantees O(log n)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of finding an element in a sorted matrix using staircase search?`, "med", "O(m + n) where m = rows, n = columns. Start from top-right or bottom-left corner, move left (if current > target) or down (if current < target). Each step eliminates a row or column. Not O(log n) because you can't binary search all dimensions simultaneously."],
  [`What is the space complexity of an iterative BFS using a queue?`, "med", "O(V) — queue may contain all vertices at the widest level of the graph. For trees: max queue size = max width of tree. For graphs: queue may hold many vertices depending on branching factor. BFS space is typically worse than DFS (DFS = O(h) vs BFS = O(width))."],
  [`What is the time complexity of computing a prefix sum array?`, "easy", "O(n). Single pass: prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + arr[i]. One loop over n elements."],
  [`What is the time complexity of a range sum query after building a prefix sum?`, "easy", "O(1). sum(l, r) = prefix[r] - prefix[l-1]. Constant time after O(n) precomputation. This is the key advantage: pay O(n) once, then O(1) per query."],
  [`What is the time complexity of Union-Find with path compression and union by rank?`, "hard", "O(α(n)) per operation (amortized), where α(n) is the inverse Ackermann function. α(n) grows so slowly that for all practical n (≤ 10^1000), α(n) ≤ 4. Practically O(1). Without optimizations: O(n) worst-case."],
  [`What is the time complexity of building a trie over k strings of length L?`, "med", "O(k × L) — each character of each string inserted once. Each node creation is O(1). Search: O(L) per string regardless of total keys. Space: O(total characters × alphabet size) worst-case (each node has many child pointers)."],
  [`What is the time complexity of the two-pointer approach on a sorted array?`, "easy", "O(n) — one pass with left and right pointers moving toward center. Each iteration advances one pointer. Total ≤ n steps. Classic example: pair sum equals target."],
  [`What does a level-order traversal of a complete binary tree visit first?`, "easy", "The root. Level-order (BFS) visits root, then left-to-right at each level. For complete binary tree of [1,2,3,4,5]: order = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]."],
  [`What is the time complexity of heapsort?`, "med", "O(n log n) — guaranteed (best, worst, average). Build heap: O(n). Extract n elements: each extraction is O(log n). Total: O(n) + O(n log n) = O(n log n). Space: O(1) in-place. Heapsort is not stable."],
  [`What is the time complexity of detecting a cycle with Floyd's tortoise and hare?`, "med", "O(n) — fast pointer moves 2 steps, slow moves 1. Within ≤ O(n) steps, fast catches slow if cycle exists. Space: O(1) — no extra data structures. The algorithm is optimal for cycle detection."],
  [`What is the time and space complexity of memoized coin change?`, "med", "Time: O(amount × n) where n = number of coin denominations. Each amount from 0 to amount checks all coins. Space: O(amount) for memo array + O(amount) recursion stack in worst case. With tabulation (bottom-up): O(amount × n) time, O(amount) space."],
  [`What is the time complexity of a sliding window over an array of size n?`, "easy", "O(n) — each element is added to the window once (right pointer moves n times) and removed at most once (left pointer moves ≤ n times). Total ≤ 2n operations = O(n). This is the key efficiency of sliding window over O(n²) brute force."],
];
